// The two rules that keep `@target(.X)` ALWAYS a body and never a declaration.
//
// Without them, telling one from the other would mean looking elsewhere in the
// module for a bare `@target` of the same name — the one non-local question the
// model otherwise avoids. Both are checked BEFORE selection, so they fire on
// every target rather than only on the one being built: a typo is an error on
// the machine that wrote it, not a surprise on whichever platform compiles it
// next.

module "main"

@target
export ok :: fn() -> i32 = 1

@target
leaky :: fn() -> i32 = 5

main :: fn() -> i32 = ok()
